    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203537": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Nursing Simulation/Skills Lab Coordinator",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Nursing Simulation/Skills Lab Coordinator Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Nursing Simulation/Skills Lab Coordinator Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Nursing Simulation/Skills Lab Coordinator Category: Administrative Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Academic Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: Aug 10, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 203537 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 11 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2026. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2026 U.S. News rankings: No. 6 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 4 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: GENERAL STATEMENT OF DUTIES: The Nursing Simulation/Skills Lab Coordinator works as part of the team in MCLA's Baccalaureate of Science in Nursing (BSN) Program and is an educational and technical expert who can develop, operate, maintain, troubleshoot, repair simulation and nursing skills lab equipment. The position works closely with nursing faculty members of the Baccalaureate of Science in Nursing (BSN) Program, supporting the Program and curriculum for the BSN major. This essential staff member is responsible for the overall day-to-day operations of the simulation/nursing skills lab focusing extensively on facilitating student learning outcomes associated with prescribed course and program outcomes, implementation of the nursing curriculum, program evaluation and improvement; supports the philosophy of nursing program and supports the mission of the College. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Associate Dean of Nursing DETAILED STATEMENT OF DUTIES: Scheduling, planning, and running simulation and nursing skills events. Preparation, cleaning, and storage of all simulation and skills lab equipment and technology before, during, and after student learning events. Maintaining simulation and skills lab inventory and recommend purchasing to Associate Dean of Nursing. If Simulation Coordinator possesses a master's degree, teaching laboratory courses may be expected. Create an educational environment which fosters innovation, responsiveness, and accountability in the lab and clinical environment. Provide program-specific expertise and is actively engaged in relevant department initiatives. Serve as a professional role model for students in the lab and clinical environment. Requirements: Hold a current Massachusetts Registered Nurse license in good standing. Minimum of a bachelor's degree, master's degree preferred. Clinical experience in a specialty field; knowledge and experience in baccalaureate education preferred. Additional Information: This is a full-time, permanent, 10-month, benefited position within the APA bargaining union. Position starts in January 2027. Salary for this position is $56,000-$68,000 per year. Employee Benefits: Health Insurance: MCLA offers a variety of HMO, POS, and PPO-type health insurance plans for eligible employees. Coverage is effective on the 1st of the month following the date of hire. Exceptions to employees hired on the 1st of the month, coverage is effective immediately. Retirement: The MA State Employee Retirement System provides eligible employees with a retirement pension following a 10-year vesting period. In lieu of the retirement pension, eligible employees may select the Optional Retirement Plan with a maximum 5% employer matching contribution. Tuition Waivers & Discounts: eligible employees, including spouses and dependent children, receive reduced tuition at MA state colleges, community colleges, and universities. Paid Time Off: employees receive 12 paid holidays and generous vacation, personal, and sick leave benefits. Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Candidates are required to submit a resume, cover letter, online application and 3 professional references. To submit application materials, click the apply now button below. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203481": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Staff Counselor",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Staff Counselor Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Staff Counselor Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Staff Counselor Category: Administrative Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Administrative &nbsp; Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: Aug 7, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 203481 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 11 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2026. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2026 U.S. News rankings: No. 6 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 4 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: GENERAL STATEMENT OF DUTIES: Provides individual and group counseling, development of treatment plans, crisis intervention, and consultation/outreach as well as psychological assessments, intakes, and appropriate referrals. Promote and support the philosophy, policies, and procedures for collegiate counseling and holistic wellness that incorporate equity, anti-oppression work, and social justice concepts and principles that support student wellbeing. Complete required clinical documentation in a timely manner. Assist in coordinating care and related client follow-up with medical professionals, off campus mental health professionals, family, and other support networks. Consult with supervisor and staff on a regular basis. May perform other related tasks that are a part of the department's operation, including developing and delivering campus communications or presentations, outreach to campus constituencies, trainings, consultations with faculty and staff, and other duties as assigned. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Reports directly to the Associate Dean for Student Wellness & Chief Wellness Officer DETAILED STATEMENT OF DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Counseling: Provide in person counseling services to MCLA students, including individual, couples and group counseling, crisis intervention, and case management. Develop and provide educational outreach programs for residence halls, campus organizations, and departments, teams, classes, or other campus groups. Provide consultation and referral services to the entire campus community, including faculty, staff, students, and family members or other support networks. Consult with staff psychiatrist in conjunction with prescribing and monitoring psychotropic medication for psychotherapy clients. Consult with families and student support networks, physicians, faculty, academic dean, residence life staff, off-campus counseling services, other campus offices and students with appropriate authorization to provide quality care to students. Collaborate with other staff members in group supervision and in developing policies and procedures for Counseling Services. Pursue opportunities for professional development including relevant college student mental health trends and topics. Conduct community-based intervention programming as requested by the campus community, including orientation sessions for new students and families, informal support spaces, psychoeducational workshop presentations, and emergency support services. Participate in ongoing active educational outreach programs. May serves as a liaison to other campus departments or academic programs. Maintain accurate and timely clinical documentation of client records in accordance with professional standards, state, and federal laws. Adhere to relevant ethical and legal standards including FERPA, HIPAA, professional governance bodies, and state boards. Some evening and weekend hours should be expected, especially involving crisis intervention rotation, expanded student clinical hours, and outreach activities. Performs other duties as assigned. Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion: Demonstrate a sensitivity to, and understanding of, the diverse academic, socioeconomic, cultural, disability, gender identity, sexual orientation, and ethnic backgrounds of MCLA's student body. Collaborate with the diversity, equity, and inclusion professionals on training and education and employ best practices to support the mental wellbeing of traditionally minoritized populations. Participate in ongoing clinical staff training within a social justice framework. Participate in assessment related to the state strategic plan for racial equity. Requirements: Hold a completed master's degree in social work, counseling, or related field from an accredited program or doctoral level degree (Ph.D. or Psy.D.) in clinical or counseling psychology from an APA accredited program. Currently licensed or eligible to be licensed as an independent mental health provider (e.g., psychologist, licensed clinical professional counselor, or licensed independent clinical social worker) by the Commonwealth of Massachusetts (i.e., at time of application, must have completed all licensure requirements including successfully passing any examinations and completing any clinical supervision hours required for licensure by the Commonwealth of Massachusetts). Massachusetts license must be obtained no later than 6 months after hire date to maintain employment. One to three years of experience in counseling, including formulation and implementation of counseling and treatment plans with young adults. Clinical skills compatible with a brief treatment model and experience in assessment, individual and group counseling, and crisis intervention. Commitment to creating a just and inclusive community, intercultural competency, and enthusiasm for working in an institution committed to access and social justice. Experience working with diverse populations and at-risk individuals. Ability to manage multiple initiatives, programs, and responsibilities while ensuring clinical excellence while providing direct services. Innovative leadership practices and exceptional interpersonal, verbal, and written communication. Ability to work collaboratively with a wide range of constituencies. Strong interpersonal skills, including oral, written, listening, and presentation skills. Use sound judgment and discretion in handling sensitive mental health issues with confidentiality and discretion. Preferred Qualifications: Previous experience working in a college counseling center or clinical experience with a college aged population is preferred. Knowledge of college student development and mental health issues specific to college students. Knowledge of higher education principles, practices, and procedures. Experience working with a diverse population in a fast-paced, stimulating environment. Interest in collaborating and working effectively with professional staff, faculty, students, and administrative staff, both on and off campus. Additional Information: This is a full-time, 10-month, permanent position within the APA bargaining union. Salary for this position is $50,000 per year. Position starts on August 31, 2026 Employee Benefits: Health Insurance: MCLA offers a variety of HMO, POS, and PPO-type health insurance plans for eligible employees. Coverage is effective on the 1st of the month following the date of hire. Exceptions to employees hired on the 1st of the month, coverage is effective immediately. Retirement: The MA State Employee Retirement System provides eligible employees with a retirement pension following a 10-year vesting period. In lieu of the retirement pension, eligible employees may select the Optional Retirement Plan with a maximum 5% employer matching contribution. Tuition Waivers & Discounts: eligible employees, including spouses and dependent children, receive reduced tuition at MA state colleges, community colleges, and universities. Paid Time Off: employees receive 12 paid holidays and generous vacation, personal, and sick leave benefits. Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Applicants are required to submit a resume, cover letter, online application, and 3 professional references. Candidates should submit application materials by clicking the apply now button below. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203470": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Neurodiversity Support Specialist",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Neurodiversity Support Specialist Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Neurodiversity Support Specialist Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Neurodiversity Support Specialist Category: Administrative Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Academic Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: Aug 7, 2026 Closes: Sep 9, 2026 - 11:05 AM EDT Type: Part-time Position ID: 203470 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 11 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2026. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2026 U.S. News rankings: No. 6 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 4 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: GENERAL STATEMENT OF DUTIES: The Neurodiversity Support Specialist supports students in navigating the interpersonal, self-advocacy, and independent living aspects of college life and beyond. Through individual and group coaching, programming, and student-centered workshops, this position helps to create an environment that will facilitate individual growth and achievement and help students to identify and pursue meaningful personal goals. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Reports to the Director of Disability Resources DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITILIES: Assist students with social behaviors and pragmatics through role playing, small group instruction, visual tools and other targeted interventions and/or incidental opportunities to improve these interactions. Help students navigate new social environments, manage independence, and self-care. Help students establish routines utilizing scripts, checklists, and other visual cues. Provide instruction with life skills such as dorm cleaning, laundry, budgeting, organizational skills, meal planning, preparation, and cooking, etc. Help students establish support networks and peer groups/relationships. Support students in identifying meaningful personal goals related to independence, connection, self-advocacy, and engagement in college and community life. Design and facilitate workshops and programming based on student-identified goals and support needs including classroom participation skills, work readiness habits, and collaborative language. Partner with students to identify strategies for managing daily responsibilities and balancing academic, social, and independent living demands. Collaborate with campus partners from onboarding through graduation to connect students with resources, opportunities, and experiences that support relationship building, self-advocacy, and independent living goals. Coordinate referral pathways with campus partners, including Residential Life and Housing, the CARE Team, and Counseling Services, to connect students with supports and services. Meet regularly with students to support progress towards identified goals. Organize outings and social events, based on student interests, to expand opportunities for social connection and participation in campus and community life. Requirements: Bachelor's degree required. Master's degree preferred Minimum of two years of experience working with young adults in educational, direct support, independent living, human services, or related settings. Experience in higher education is strongly preferred. Additional Information: This is a part-time, non-benefited,10-month grant-funded position, working up to 18.5 hours per week. This position requires afternoon and evening availability with occasional weekend responsibilities. Position starts on October 1, 2026 and goes through July 31, 2027 with the option for renewal. Salary for this position is $32.50 per hour. Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Applicants are required to submit a resume, cover letter, online application, and 3 professional references. To submit application materials, please click the apply now button below. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203161": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: User Services Desktop Administrator",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: User Services Desktop Administrator Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: User Services Desktop Administrator Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details User Services Desktop Administrator Category: Administrative Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Administrative &nbsp; Locations: NORTH ADAMS, MA Posted: Jul 29, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 203161 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 11 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2026. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2026 U.S. News rankings: No. 6 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 4 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: GENERAL STATEMENT OF DUTIES: Responsible for the upkeep, configuration, and reliable operation of computer systems, computer management software and their peripherals. Be able to ensure that the uptime, performance, resources, and security of the computers he or she manages meet the needs of the users, without exceeding the budget. This position includes the planning, implementation, coordination and maintenance of all Desktop Support Service activities administered by User Services. The duties of this position may also be to acquire, install, or upgrade computer components and software; provide routine automation; maintain security policies; troubleshoot; train staff; or offer technical support for projects. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Reports to the Assistant Director of User Services SUPERVISION EXERCISED: None DETAILED STATEMENT OF DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Install, configure, deploy and maintain hardware devices including computers, hard drives, scanners, cameras, printers, and other peripherals. Create and maintain a variety of computer images for Windows and Mac OS systems. Work with vendors to troubleshoot and resolve issues. Be the primary technician in charge of the college's laptop warranty services and maintain certifications required to perform such services. Provide members of the MCLA community with installation, upgrade, modification, and maintenance of their computer-based applications software. Administer computer management software to effectively manage all endpoint devices and virtual desktops. Provide onsite technical assistance for faculty, staff, and students. Identify, assign and/or escalate Help Desk issues to appropriate IT resource, and/or vendor, to ensure timely resolution of support requests. Alert Assistant Director of User Services or other appropriate IT staff/managers to patterns and global problems. Assist in maintaining records for equipment in use and equipment that is recycled or discarded. Be responsible for the care, redistribution, and storage of equipment. Maintain accurate inventory information for all computer desktops, laptops, and peripherals. Contribute to the creation of user documentation and training materials as assigned. Assist the Assistant Director of User Services to respond to calls, e-mail, and in-person technical assistance from faculty, staff, and students. Provide management to the Student Help Desk when needed to support the Assistant Director of User Services. Requirements: A Bachelor's degree or four years of professional experience servicing computers and peripherals. Experience in managing and documenting a community of hundreds of devices.  Experience using enterprise endpoint management tools such as Microsoft Endpoint Manager/Intune, JAMF, MDT and AD. Expertise with the Windows operating system including GUI customization, patching, driver installation/updating, group policies and the command line. Experience with managing virtual desktops in Microsoft Azure Virtual Desktop (AVD) is a plus. Experience with Mac OS X and Linux a plus. Excellent skills with tools used for virus, spyware and malware detection and removal. Experience using remote support software to diagnose and resolve problems. A working knowledge of TCP/IP and ETHERNET and wireless network communications; including configuration methods in most popular devices. A working knowledge of computer applications software including Microsoft Office 365, Microsoft Defender, Adobe Creative Cloud, web browsers, and endpoint driver management software. The ability to develop scripts for the purpose of administering endpoint devices is a plus. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Ability to lift and transport components weighing up to 50 pounds. The position works, or has the potential to work on, the Criminal Justice Information Services system and is subject to periodic criminal background checks and fingerprinting. Additional Information: This is a full-time, permanent, benefited position within the APA bargaining union. The salary range for this position is $60,000-$65,000 per year. Employee Benefits: Health Insurance: MCLA offers a variety of HMO, POS, and PPO-type health insurance plans for eligible employees. Coverage is effective on the 1st of the month following the date of hire. Exceptions to employees hired on the 1st of the month, coverage is effective immediately. Retirement: The MA State Employee Retirement System provides eligible employees with a retirement pension following a 10-year vesting period. In lieu of the retirement pension, eligible employees may select the Optional Retirement Plan with a maximum 5% employer matching contribution. Tuition Waivers & Discounts: eligible employees, including spouses and dependent children, receive reduced tuition at MA state colleges, community colleges, and universities. Paid Time Off: employees receive 12 paid holidays and generous vacation, personal, and sick leave benefits. Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Applicants are required to submit a resume, cover letter, online application, and 3 professional references to be considered for this position. Candidates should click the apply now button to submit application materials. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=202857": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Vice President of Institutional Advancement",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Vice President of Institutional Advancement Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Vice President of Institutional Advancement Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Vice President of Institutional Advancement Category: Administrative Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Administrative &nbsp; Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: Jul 22, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 202857 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 11 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2026. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2026 U.S. News rankings: No. 6 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 4 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: POSITION SUMMARY: The Vice President for Institutional Advancement (VPIA) serves as the College's lead advancement officer and a member of the President's Cabinet. Reporting directly to the President, the VPIA provides visionary, strategic, and operational leadership for all institutional advancement activities, including fundraising, alumni and constituent engagement, advancement services, foundation relations, donor stewardship, grants development, communications, public relations, and campaign planning and execution. The VPIA develops and implements a comprehensive advancement strategy that advances the College's mission, vision, strategic priorities, and long-term financial sustainability. The position is responsible for building and strengthening relationships with alumni, donors, corporations, foundations, government agencies, community leaders, and other key stakeholders while fostering a culture of philanthropy and engagement across the institution. The VPIA also serves as President and Chief Executive Officer of the MCLA Foundation, Inc., working closely with the Foundation Board of Directors and College leadership to increase philanthropic support and enhance institutional visibility and reputation. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Reports directly to the President. SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Provides leadership, supervision, professional development, and performance management for all personnel assigned to the Institutional Advancement division, including professional, classified, and student employees. ESSENTIAL DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Strategic Advancement Leadership 1 Develop, implement, and evaluate a comprehensive institutional advancement strategy aligned with the College's mission, strategic plan, and institutional priorities. 2. Serve as the College's chief strategist for philanthropy, donor engagement, and resource development, ensuring the integration of fundraising, alumni relations, communications, stewardship, and advancement operations. 3. Advise the President, Cabinet, and Foundation Board on fundraising opportunities, philanthropic trends, constituent engagement strategies, and advancement best practices. 4. Establish annual and multi-year fundraising goals and key performance indicators, utilizing data analytics and benchmarking to evaluate effectiveness and support continuous improvement. 5. Represent the College as a member of the MA State University Advancement Group Fundraising and Resource Development 1. Lead all aspects of the College's comprehensive fundraising program, including: Major and principal gifts Annual giving Planned giving Capital and comprehensive campaigns Corporate and foundation relations Government and public-sector funding opportunities Foundation grants Special fundraising initiatives and events 2. Maintain an active portfolio of major and principal gift prospects and personally cultivate, solicit, negotiate, and steward significant philanthropic investments. 3. Work closely with the President, Foundation Board members, alumni leaders, and volunteers to identify, cultivate, solicit, and steward transformational gifts and strategic partnerships. 4. Direct campaign planning, readiness assessment, implementation, and evaluation for major fundraising initiatives and capital projects. Foundation Leadership and Governance 1. Serve as President and Chief Executive Officer of the MCLA Foundation, Inc. 2. Report regularly to the Foundation Board of Directors regarding financial performance, fundraising results, strategic initiatives, and organizational effectiveness. 3. Support the work of the Foundation Board, Corporators, and committees by developing agendas, facilitating strategic discussions, and ensuring implementation of board priorities. 4. Ensure Foundation operations, investment activities, and stewardship practices comply with all applicable legal, regulatory, fiduciary, and ethical standards. 5. Manage Foundation owned properties. Alumni and Constituent Engagement 1. Provide strategic leadership for alumni engagement initiatives that strengthen alumni participation, volunteerism, advocacy, lifelong affinity, and philanthropic support. 2. Develop programs that enhance connections among alumni, students, families, community partners, and friends of the College. 3. Oversee donor stewardship and recognition programs that ensure meaningful engagement, accountability, and long-term donor retention. Communications and External Relations 1. Provide executive oversight for advancement-related communications, publications, marketing efforts, digital engagement, social media strategies, and donor communications. 2. Collaborate with campus leadership to strengthen the College's public image, reputation, and visibility among internal and external audiences. 3. Serve as an ambassador for the College throughout the region and Commonwealth by cultivating relationships with business leaders, elected officials, philanthropic organizations, and community stakeholders. 4. Supervision of campus Conferencing & Events program. Advancement Operations and Data Strategy Ensure effective advancement services operations, including Prospect research Database management Gift processing Data integrity Reporting and analytics Campaign tracking Stewardship systems 1. Utilize data, technology, and predictive analytics to improve donor engagement, portfolio management, fundraising performance, and resource allocation. 2. Maintain policies, procedures, and systems that support ethical fundraising, donor confidentiality, and exemplary constituent service. Leadership and Administration 1. Recruit, develop, mentor, and evaluate a high-performing advancement team characterized by collaboration, accountability, innovation, and continuous learning. 2. Develop and administer divisional budgets and resources in accordance with institutional and Foundation policies. 3. Foster a culture of philanthropy and engagement across the campus community by partnering with faculty, staff, administrators, students, and volunteers. 4. Advance the College's mission, vision, values, and strategic priorities through leadership, decision-making, and relationship building. 5. Attend and partner with the other MA State University Advancement Officers. 6. Ensure effective communication within the Institutional Advancement division and throughout the College. 7. Serve as a member of the President's Cabinet, participating in institutional planning, policy development, and strategic decision-making. 8. Ensure that gifts align with the institutional strategic plan. 9. Perform other related duties as assigned by the President. Requirements: Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution. Minimum of eight (8) years of progressively responsible leadership experience in higher education advancement, development, nonprofit management, or a closely related field, including significant fundraising responsibility. Demonstrated success personally cultivating, soliciting, closing, and stewarding major and principal gifts. Proven experience leading comprehensive fundraising programs, including annual giving, major gifts, planned giving, corporate and foundation relations, campaign management, donor stewardship, and alumni engagement. Experience working effectively with governing boards, foundation boards, volunteers, and executive leadership. Strong financial and operational management skills, including budget development, resource allocation, and performance management. Demonstrated success using data, metrics, and technology to drive decision-making and improve advancement outcomes. Exceptional interpersonal, relationship-building, communication, presentation, and negotiation skills. Thorough understanding of fundraising ethics, donor stewardship, charitable giving regulations, and nonprofit governance practices. Ability to inspire confidence, build consensus, and work effectively with diverse constituencies, including students, faculty, staff, alumni, donors, community leaders, and public officials. Experience utilizing advancement CRM systems and fundraising technologies; familiarity with Raiser's Edge or comparable advancement platforms preferred. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree or other advanced professional degree. Experience serving in a senior advancement leadership role within higher education. Experience directing comprehensive or capital campaigns with multimillion-dollar fundraising goals. Knowledge of public higher education governance, finance, policies, and procedures. Demonstrated experience overseeing integrated advancement functions, including communications, marketing, alumni relations, advancement services, and foundation operations. Leadership Competencies The successful candidate will demonstrate: Integrity, professionalism, and sound judgment. Strategic and visionary leadership. Commitment to collaboration and shared governance. Entrepreneurial thinking and innovation. Political acumen and relationship-building ability. Accountability for results and measurable outcomes. Commitment to diversity, belonging, and institutional excellence. Flexibility, resilience and adaptability in a dynamic environment. Additional Information: This is a full-time, permanent, benefited position starting on 1/04/2027. Salary for this position is between $200,000 and $210,000 per year. Applications received by 8/31/2026 will receive preferential treatment. Employee Benefits: Health Insurance: MCLA offers a variety of HMO, POS, and PPO-type health insurance plans for eligible employees. Coverage is effective on the 1st of the month following the date of hire. Exceptions to employees hired on the 1st of the month, coverage is effective immediately. Retirement: The MA State Employee Retirement System provides eligible employees with a retirement pension following a 10-year vesting period. In lieu of the retirement pension, eligible employees may select the Optional Retirement Plan with a maximum 5% employer matching contribution. Tuition Waivers & Discounts : eligible employees, including spouses and dependent children, receive reduced tuition at MA state colleges, community colleges, and universities. Paid Time Off: employees receive 12 paid holidays and generous vacation, personal, and sick leave benefits. Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Applicants are required to submit a resume, cover letter, online application, and 3 professional references to be considered for this position. Candidates should click the apply now button to submit application materials. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203458": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Chief of Staff for Diversity and Equity",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Chief of Staff for Diversity and Equity Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Chief of Staff for Diversity and Equity Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Chief of Staff for Diversity and Equity Category: Administrative Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Administrative &nbsp; Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: Aug 6, 2026 Closes: Sep 18, 2026 - 11:59 PM EDT Type: Full-time Position ID: 203458 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each, of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 10 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2025, after earning the No. 7 spot the prior three years. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2025 U.S. News rankings: No. 5 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 2 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well school's graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: GENERAL STATEMENT OF DUTIES: The Chief of Staff for Diversity and Equity serves as a member of the President's Cabinet and provides strategic leadership to advance the College's commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging (DEIB). The position collaborates with academic and administrative leaders to develop, implement, and assess policies, practices, and initiatives that promote access, inclusion, student success, and institutional excellence. The Chief of Staff for Diversity and Equity works to establish and sustain responsive institutional structures and practices that support all members of the MCLA community, with particular emphasis on students and communities that have been historically underserved or excluded from higher education. The position partners with college leadership, faculty, staff, and shared governance bodies to ensure that diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging are embedded in institutional planning, decision-making, and daily operations. In addition, the role supports efforts to advance equitable access and success across academic programs and high-impact educational practices while fostering a campus culture grounded in dignity, mutual respect, and belonging. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Reports directly to the President. SUPERVISION EXERCISED: This position does not provide direct personnel supervision. The incumbent works collaboratively with faculty, staff, administrators, and campus committees across all divisions and serves as a ex-officio member of the Committee on Diversity, Equity, Inclusion, and Belonging. The position is housed within the President's Office. DETAILED STATEMENT OF DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: Coordinate and facilitate cross-campus initiatives that advance the College's commitment to diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging. Partner with the President's Chief of Staff to support the Cabinet and Leadership Team in advancing institution-wide equity goals through strategic planning, communication, collaboration, and team development. Collaborate with offices across campus to develop, implement, and assess training and professional development programs that strengthen competencies related to equity, inclusion, and belonging and enhance effectiveness in working across differences and with diverse populations. Work with Human Resources and executive leadership to sustain equitable recruitment, hiring, onboarding, and retention practices that support the development of a workforce reflective of and responsive to the College's student population. Partner with campus-wide committees, including but not limited to Strategic Planning, the Diversity, Equity, Inclusion, and Belonging Committee, and the Racial Equity and Justice Institute, to develop, implement, and assess initiatives that advance institutional equity and belonging and align with accreditation standards, strategic priorities, and the mission of MCLA. Review and assess institutional policies, programs, practices, services, and resources through a lens of access, equity, engagement, student success, and belonging. Analyze and leverage institutional data and assessment tools, including the Campus Climate Survey and NSSE, to inform decision-making and improve enrollment, persistence, engagement, and graduation outcomes for all students, particularly those historically excluded from higher education. Identify policies, practices, and conditions that impede the creation of an inclusive and equitable campus environment and work collaboratively, under the President's guidance, to develop and implement solutions. Lead campus-wide programs, initiatives, and communications that foster an inclusive and welcoming community by collaborating with faculty and staff on activities such as MCLA's Day of Dialogue, Lavender Lecture Series, Summit for Racial Equity, and other related programs. Perform other duties as assigned by the President in support of institutional priorities related to diversity, equity, inclusion, belonging, and student success. Requirements: MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Master's degree from an accredited institution. Five to ten years of progressively responsible leadership experience in diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging initiatives within higher education, nonprofit organizations, government agencies, or other public-serving institutions. Demonstrated success leading organization-wide DEIB initiatives, programs, and strategic efforts. Demonstrated experience using data and assessment results to inform decision-making and drive organizational improvement. Demonstrated experience facilitating dialogue, workshops, training, or educational programs in higher education or comparable settings. Demonstrated ability to work effectively, inclusively, and collaboratively with individuals from diverse backgrounds, identities, perspectives, and experiences. Experience teaching, training, coaching, or facilitating learning and skill development, preferably in a college or university setting. Demonstrated ability to analyze complex information, apply critical thinking, and develop practical solutions to institutional challenges. Demonstrated ability to build and maintain effective working relationships across diverse constituencies. Demonstrated excellence in written and verbal communication, project management, and organizational effectiveness. Demonstrated fiscal stewardship, budget management experience, and sound resource allocation practices. Demonstrated ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise sound professional judgment. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Earned doctorate or other terminal degree from an accredited institution. Ten or more years of relevant leadership experience. Current and relevant certifications, training, or professional development in areas such as cultural competency, restorative practices, conflict resolution, organizational change management, or related disciplines that support inclusive and equitable post-secondary educational environments. DESIRED LEADERSHIP CHARACTERISTICS: The MCLA community has identified the following leadership characteristics as important for the Chief of Staff for Diversity and Equity: Demonstrates action-oriented leadership informed by scholarship, reflection, and continuous learning. Values collaboration, relationship building, and transparent communication. Respects and encourages diverse perspectives, ideas, and approaches to problem-solving. Actively listens to others and communicates ideas openly, respectfully, and constructively. Fosters trust through transparency, integrity, and accountability. Demonstrates a commitment to equity, inclusion, and social justice. Approaches student success from a growth-oriented and asset-based perspective. Additional Information: This is a full-time, permanent, benefitted position starting in January 2027. There is some flexibility with the start date. Salary for this position is $115,000-$125,000 per year. Employee Benefits: Health Insurance: MCLA offers a variety of HMO, POS, and PPO-type health insurance plans for eligible employees. Coverage is effective on the 1st of the month following the date of hire. Exceptions to employees hired on the 1st of the month, coverage is effective immediately. Retirement: The MA State Employee Retirement System provides eligible employees with a retirement pension following a 10-year vesting period. In lieu of the retirement pension, eligible employees may select the Optional Retirement Plan with a maximum 5% employer matching contribution. Tuition Waivers & Discounts: eligible employees, including spouses and dependent children, receive reduced tuition at MA state colleges, community colleges, and universities. Paid Time Off: employees receive 12 paid holidays and generous vacation, personal, and sick leave benefits. Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Candidates are required to submit a resume, cover letter, online application and 3 professional references. To submit application materials, click the apply now button below. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203542": {
      "title": "Executive Director of Public Safety & Chief of Police",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Executive Director of Public Safety & Chief of Police Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Executive Director of Public Safety & Chief of Police Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Executive Director of Public Safety & Chief of Police Category: Administrative Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: Aug 10, 2026 Closes: Sep 7, 2026 - 11:59 PM EDT Type: Full-time Position ID: 203542 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 11 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2026. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2026 U.S. News rankings: No. 6 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 4 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: GENERAL STATEMENT OF DUTIES: The Executive Director of Public Safety and Chief of Police (Chief) is the administrative head of the college's Department of Public Safety and Campus Police. The Chief is responsible for the administration, planning, organization, direction, coordination and overall supervision of the operation of the department. This includes community policing, law enforcement, crime prevention, fire safety and prevention, traffic control and enforcement, emergency and critical incident planning, and all other related services concerned with the preservation of life and property for a campus community. This includes students, employees, alumnae, and visitors wherever there is a campus footprint and presence. Situated in the division of Student Affairs, the Chief leads the department with a philosophy that includes student development and education, collegiality and collaboration, and equity. SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Reports to the Vice President for Student Affairs SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Supervises all employees of the Department of Public Safety and Campus Police which includes full-time and part-time Campus Police Officers, dispatchers, administrative staff, and any student employees assigned to the department. DETAILED STATEMENT OF DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES: General Administrative Duties 20% Organize, direct and evaluate all department activities. Develop departmental policies and procedures which relate directly to achievement of the department mission, as well as the mission statement of Student Affairs and MCLA. Prepare, submit and administer the annual budget for the department. Conducts planning and research activities relating to all aspects of public safety in a college environment.  This includes the research and acquisition of grants and other funding sources. Maintain adequate records of all incidents relating to health, safety, or the protection of life and property necessary for compliance with local, state and federal laws and regulations. Prepares, submits and disseminates crime statistics specific to college property and surrounding campus areas.  (Clery Report) Make significant contributions to state and Federal mandates with regards to new regulations and laws for key areas of compliance through reporting, data gathering and assessment, and collaboration (ie: Title IX) Develops, plans and organizes the automation of all department records.  This includes the supervision of the use of the National Crime Information Center computer (LEAPS), department computers used to manage crime statistics, arrest information, personnel information, training records, department daily log reports, incident reports, uniform crime reports, monthly statistical reports of crime submitted to the Criminal History Systems Board, CORI/SORI information certification and records keeping, and any other records management regulated by local, state and federal guidelines and laws. Provide and maintain necessary supportive equipment, vehicles, office supplies and other related equipment required to complete the department mission. Serve on the Student Affairs division senior team; regularly attend staff meetings, lead trainings and provide pertinent updates, provide guidance to the administrative on-call rotation members, and support the mission, vision, and values of the division. Work with other college staff and officials to provide and promote an understanding of the department goals and priorities as they relate to the overall mission of MCLA. Personnel Responsibilities 25% Ensure the proper recruitment, training, and supervision of Campus Police Officers, dispatchers and other department members. Provide access to and support of departmental staff in obtaining in-service training that will ensure that the exercise of their authority will be in compliance with moral, ethical and legal standards, as well as MCLA policies and local, state and federal guidelines, including the Constitution of the United States. Supervise department personnel in accordance with union, college rules and regulations and human resource employment policies to ensure strict adherence to the highest standards of professional conduct. Ensure all personnel evaluations and reviews for department employees are completed in areas of quantity and quality of work, work habits, work attitude, work relationships and supervisory potential and professional development.  Develop and implement personnel goals and professional development based on these evaluations that are in line with the department mission, the Student Affairs mission and the mission of MCLA. Receive and process personnel grievances in accordance with union and college human resource policies. Develop and manage proper patterns of department personnel scheduling and deployment to meet the daily operational needs of the college. Enforcement and Prevention Responsibilities 25% Develop and conduct educational programs for faculty, staff, students and the local community regarding issues of personal and community health and safety such as crime prevention tips and fire safety practices. Assist with the development, implementation and coordination of campus emergency response procedures.  Fulfill the duties and responsibilities as the Incident Commander for actual or potential critical incidents through use of Incident Command System protocols.  Convenes the campus emergency response teams when appropriate. Develop and maintain formal and informal communications and relationships with municipal, state and federal law enforcement agencies, fire agencies, emergency medical services and other agencies concerned with the protection of life and property. Request the assistance of local, state and federal agencies, as may be necessary, to support the mission of the department and the protection of life or property on campus.  Establish unified commands in accordance with Incident Command System protocols when required. Direct and control the continuous patrol of college property by campus police officers to prevent crime and to protect life and property, enforce local, state and federal laws, as well as college rules and regulations, and render service to the college community and its guests, all with the highest standards of professionalism possible. Maintain the effective enforcement of established parking controls. Manage all types of investigations related to the health, welfare, safety, security and protection of life and property occurring on campus. Communicates with all college community members through the use of public safety announcements and alerts, information that is critical to maintaining the highest levels of public safety and security. Life Safety Responsibilities 20% Organize, plan and coordinate the college's safety and security programs in consultation with Directors, Coordinators, committees and Senior Management of the college. Develop procedures that supplement and support campus wide emergency response. Review, investigate and analyze reported safety, security, health and welfare violations or conditions and recommend corrective actions for deficiencies. Plan, develop, recommend and implement policies, procedures and programs related to safety and security practices applicable to all college community members and its guests. Initiate, coordinate and conduct first aid classes, fire safety demonstrations and programs specifically related to the safety and security of college employees. Coordinate programs related to fire alarm tests and building evacuation drills on campus and within the control of college affiliated groups and ensure that necessary records and documentation of such are maintained. Coordinate, conduct and report on safety inspections in consultation with local, state and federal agencies related to safety, security and other health and welfare issues, to the college administration and affiliated departments. Keep abreast of and review the newest trends, latest technologies, and revisions of laws and regulations surrounding all facets of safety and security.  Serve as the college's representative at meetings dealings with these topics. Equity, Diversity, Inclusion, and Justice 5% Coordinate the college's effort to address the recommendations in the 2021 Racially Just Policing Model Policy released by the Massachusetts ACLU, Bridgewater State University, and SIDLEY. Lead college efforts to be in compliance with State and Federal police reform mandates. Demonstrate and document efforts to recruit and retain a diverse workforce representative of the campus population served. Oversight of the College's Motor pool Program 5% Administrative oversight Fiscal responsibility Software management Training and Education Schedule Coordination Maintenance Liaison with facilities and athletics Requirements: MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED AT HIRE: Candidates must possess a minimum of seven to ten years of progressive law enforcement experience with 4 years of supervisory experiences as a Sergeant or above, as a full-time sworn police officer in good standing on a campus law enforcement department or other equivalent Police Department. Experience and knowledge of campus security and law enforcement policies and procedures, crime prevention, investigations, training for law enforcement personnel, life safety programming, college operations and traffic and parking control management. Knowledge of budget operations and management specific to a law enforcement / public safety agency. Thorough knowledge and understanding of state law, rules, regulations and ability to interpret new laws, rules and regulations correctly and accurately is required. Appointees must be eligible for appointment as a Special State Police Officer under Chapter 22c §63 of the Massachusetts General Laws. Must have successfully completed a Massachusetts Basic Police Training Program from an accredited police academy, approved by the Municipal Police Training Committee (MPTC) or have successfully completed training that is equivalent to or greater than that of a Massachusetts police officer at a comparable level of experience to obtain an academy exemption or waiver through the MPTC. Possession of a current motor vehicle operator's license is required. Ability to gain and maintain MA POST certification. Ability to gain and maintain MA MPTC qualifications. Ability to obtain and maintain an MA License to Carry (LTC) a firearm. Must be a U.S. citizen. Ability to qualify with departmentally issued firearm(s) and all departmentally issued less - lethal weapons. Ability to obtain or possess CPR/First Responder certification and be trained in the operation of Automated External Defibrillator. Be able to successfully complete a background check. Be able to successfully complete and pass psychological evaluation. Be able to successfully complete and pass drug screening. Communicate effectively, both orally and in writing. Ability to obtain certification as a Law Enforcement Administration System Computer Operator after hire. Computer literacy in law enforcement technology including word processing, LEAPS, ID equipment, various alarm systems/computers and other security related software. Ability to train staff on department policies and procedures. Ability to work at any hour and in all weather conditions. Ability to maintain professionalism and acceptable personal demeanor under periods of stress, verbal and physical abuse, danger, and emotional tension. Ability to physically perform the essential functions of a job. Demonstrated ability to effectively supervise and coordinate the activities of police and security personnel of lower grade while maintaining good working relationships. Training and experience in criminal investigations. Ability to maintain good order and acceptable personal demeanor under periods of stress, verbal and physical abuse, danger, and emotional tension. Strong organizational skills. Demonstrated experience serving and supporting diverse populations. Thorough understanding and demonstrated experiences of equity and inclusivity as they pertain to department philosophy for personnel management and day-to-day execution of responsibilities. Any candidate who has sustained disciplinary action during the previous twelve (12) months and/or on any form of disciplinary probation shall be considered ineligible to apply. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: A bachelor's degree in criminal justice or related field from a four-year institution. Knowledge of and experience with higher education principles, practices and procedures. Working knowledge and understanding of Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act), 2008 Higher Education and Opportunity Act (HEOA), 2013 Violence Against Women Reauthorization Act (VAWA), and Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972. Experience with managing in a unionized workplace. ENVIRONMENTAL WORKING CONDITIONS: 75% Indoor and office related duties 25% Outdoor related duties Additional Information: This is a full-time, permanent benefited position. This position is expected to ensure coverage, either through designees or self, 24/7, 365 days a year. This position is a mandatory reporter for the purposes of Title IX and Equal Opportunity. The salary for this position is $115,000-$125,000 per year. Employee Benefits: Health Insurance: MCLA offers a variety of HMO, POS, and PPO-type health insurance plans for eligible employees. Coverage is effective on the 1st of the month following the date of hire. Exceptions to employees hired on the 1st of the month, coverage is effective immediately. Retirement: The MA State Employee Retirement System provides eligible employees with a retirement pension following a 10-year vesting period. In lieu of the retirement pension, eligible employees may select the Optional Retirement Plan with a maximum 5% employer matching contribution. Tuition Waivers & Discounts: eligible employees, including spouses and dependent children, receive reduced tuition at MA state colleges, community colleges, and universities. Paid Time Off: employees receive 12 paid holidays and generous vacation, personal, and sick leave benefits. Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Candidates are required to submit a resume, cover letter, online application and 3 professional references. To submit application materials, click the apply now button below. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=201294": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty-Fine and Performing Arts",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty-Fine and Performing Arts Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty-Fine and Performing Arts Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Adjunct Faculty-Fine and Performing Arts Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Academic Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: Jun 8, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Temporary Position ID: 201294 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 11 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2026. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2026 U.S. News rankings: No. 6 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 4 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: The Department of Fine and Performing Arts invites applications for its Arts Management Adjunct Faculty Pool. We are seeking adjunct instructors to teach any of the following courses: Introduction to Arts Management (Fall 26, Spring 27) Performing Arts Management (Fall 26), Advanced Performing Arts Management (Spring 27) Grants and Fundraising (Spring 27), Financial Management (Spring 27), Career and Portfolio Workshop (Spring 27). MCLA seeks adjunct instructors committed to high-quality undergraduate teaching, student mentoring, and advancing the college's ongoing commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion in the curriculum. Candidates with a strong commitment to working with a diverse student population are particularly encouraged to apply. Requirements: Graduate degree Work experience-Suitable work experience may be considered a viable substitute for a graduate degree Additional Information: These are part-time, temporary, semester-to-semester appointments (Fall and Spring), beginning Fall 2026. Positions are filled as course needs arise each semester. Salary for this position is $3,500-$7,480 per course Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Applicants should electronically submit an application by clicking on the APPLY NOW button below. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=198734": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Nursing Faculty Pool",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Adjunct Nursing Faculty Pool Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Nursing Faculty Pool Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Adjunct Nursing Faculty Pool Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Academic Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: Mar 31, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Position ID: 198734 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 11 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2026. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2026 U.S. News rankings: No. 6 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 4 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: The Department of Health Professions invites applications for nursing adjunct faculty pool. These are semester-to-semester positions (Fall and Spring), beginning Fall 2026. Candidates must have a minimum degree of Bachelor's in Nursing (matriculated into an MSN program), but a Masters in Nursing or Doctorate in Nursing is preferred. The adjunct nursing faculty pool positions will teach didactic (lecture), laboratory or clinical nursing content in MCLA's new Bachelor of Science in Nursing program. MCLA seeks a faculty committed to high quality teaching, undergraduate mentoring and who will make contributions to MCLA's ongoing commitment to bring diversity, equity, and inclusion to the curriculum. Faculty are expected to maintain current knowledge of the discipline and educational methodologies by maintaining an active, unencumbered Massachusetts Registered Nurse License, and by participating in continuing education programs and professional growth activities. Furthermore, these individuals will be expected to maintain current knowledge of program policies, procedures, and student progress, and maintain confidentiality in accordance with program policy. Faculty may be requested to perform other tasks or duties to support the program. The Nursing program is housed in a newly renovated wing of the Berkshire Medical Center North Adams Campus and has a new, fully equipped simulation lab. Requirements: Required Qualifications: Minimum of a Bachelor's in Nursing (matriculated into an MSN program). Master's degree in Nursing or Doctorate in Nursing or related field is preferred. A current, unencumbered Massachusetts Registered Nurse license in good standing. A minimum of 2 years clinical experience or its equivalent in the last 5 years. Special expertise in mental health, pediatrics, maternal-child health, acute care and/or long-term care Preferred Qualifications Knowledge and experience in baccalaureate education. Demonstrated teaching ability at the undergraduate level. Demonstrated commitment to inclusive pedagogies and classroom practices. Additional Information: This is a part time, temporary position within the MSCA union. Salary for this position is $1,166.66-$2,493.49/credit. Pay is based on number of semesters previously taught. Position starts on 9/08/2026. Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Applicants should electronically submit an application by clicking on the APPLY NOW button below. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=170978": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty Pool",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty Pool - Math Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Adjunct Faculty Pool - Math Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Adjunct Faculty Pool - Math Category: Faculty Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Academic Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: Dec 14, 2023 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Temporary Position ID: 170978 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each, of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 10 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2025, after earning the No. 7 spot the prior three years. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2025 U.S. News rankings: No. 5 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 2 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: The Department of Mathematics seeks applications for a part-time faculty member to teach mathematics or data science courses. Potential courses may include introductory courses such as Introduction to Statistics or Math for Liberal Arts, or upper-level math courses in the applicant's area of expertise. This adjunct position would be for one semester, with an opportunity for renewal. The instructor must have a combination of education and professional experience and expertise in the subject area. Examples of Duties Deliver instruction appropriate to the topic, course description, agreed-upon learning outcomes, academic level and student audience. Submit syllabi, grades and other reports on time. Communicate effectively with students and faculty in the department. Provide assistance to students by appointment. Requirements: Experience commensurate with course topics. Master's degree or higher in a related field is strongly preferred. Previous teaching experience at the college level is strongly preferred. Additional Information: Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Barbara Chaput, Chief Human Resources Officer. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Please note that all part-time instructor postings are for adjunct pools. Positions may or may not be immediately available, however whenever openings occur, department chairs will turn to the adjunct pools to fill the openings. Applicants to these pools will not hear back on their status unless an opening occurs and the Department Chair decides to interview that candidate to possibly fill the new opening. Applicants should electronically submit a cover letter and curriculum vitae. Questions should be directed via email to the Department Chair: Dr. Liz Hartung E.Hartung@mcla.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203699": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Campus Police Officer I",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Campus Police Officer I Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Campus Police Officer I Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Campus Police Officer I Category: Other Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Miscellaneous &nbsp; Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: Aug 13, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 203699 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each, of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 10 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2025, after earning the No. 7 spot the prior three years. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2025 U.S. News rankings: No. 5 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 2 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: The Department of Public Safety/Campus Police at Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts (MCLA) invites applications for a Campus Police Officer I.  This is an armed position, Officer will patrol the campus buildings, grounds, and areas adjacent to the College, enforcing state and federal laws and MCLA rules and regulations.  This Officer will also ensure the safety, security and well-being of the College community through: investigating crimes and other incidents; restraining and/or arresting suspects when necessary; completing crime and incident reports; providing assistance to courts in prosecution cases; and performing other related work in accordance with campus needs. Requirements: Eligibility for appointment as a Massachusetts Special State Police Officer under Massachusetts General Law, Chapter 22C, Section 63 and must obtain Special State Police Powers from the Massachusetts State Police Must have graduated from a Massachusetts Special State Police Officer (SSPO) Academy OR Must have graduated from the Massachusetts Municipal Police Training Committee (MPTC) full-time Police Academy or other full-time reciprocal Police Academy If the candidate is not Police Academy trained, that candidate must be physically fit and able to successfully complete a full-time MPTC or SSPO training academy, to include all perquisites (which includes physical endurance testing). Failure to meet the entrance requirements or to successfully complete the course of training shall result in dismissal or, for lateral appointment within the bargaining unit, return to previously held position in accordance with the AFSCME Agreement. Must be able to pass the following pre-employment examinations: fit-for-duty physical and psychological assessments under the auspices of the Campus Police Department, as well as a thorough background check to include Criminal Offender Records Information Must have a criminal history free of ANY felony convictions Must have and retain a valid driver's license Must have or be able to obtain and retain a Massachusetts license to carry a firearm Must be able to stand and or walk for prolonged periods of time; while wearing issued and approved equipment (duty belt, protective body armor etc.). Ability to lift heavy objects or persons and possess physical stamina and endurance Must be able to work with diverse populations and have the ability to deal with young adults, faculty, and staff in a multicultural, multiracial environment Must possess excellent written and oral communication skills Ability to obtain a CPR/First Responder certification and training in the operation of Automated External Defibrillator Ability to successfully complete initial and annual firearms qualification training Ability to successfully complete annual in-service training requirements Computer literacy in the department's law enforcement technology preferred, including: word processing, LEAPS, ID equipment, various alarm systems/computers, and other security-related software Ability to obtain certification as a Law Enforcement Administration System Computer Operator Ability to complete Incident Command System training A basic knowledge and understanding of police procedures and how those procedures are adapted to an educational community, preferred Ability to work varied work schedules and shifts based on assignment and the needs of the Department/College Must successfully complete the required Field Training Officer Program Additional Information: The shift for this position is Midnight-8am Sunday-Monday and Thursday-Saturday with Tuesday and Wednesday off. This is a full-time, benefit-eligible, (Grade 16, Step 1) position in the AFSCME bargaining union.  Biweekly salary is $2,268.91. Weekend and shift differential eligible. Preference may be given to applicants who have graduated from a full-time Massachusetts Police Academy. Employee Benefits: Health Insurance: MCLA offers a variety of HMO, POS, and PPO-type health insurance plans for eligible employees. Coverage is effective on the 1st of the month following the date of hire. Exceptions to employees hired on the 1st of the month, coverage is effective immediately. Retirement: The MA State Employee Retirement System provides eligible employees with a retirement pension following a 10-year vesting period. In lieu of the retirement pension, eligible employees may select the Optional Retirement Plan with a maximum 5% employer matching contribution. Tuition Waivers & Discounts: eligible employees, including spouses and dependent children, receive reduced tuition at MA state colleges, community colleges, and universities. Paid Time Off: employees receive 12 paid holidays and generous vacation, personal, and sick leave benefits. Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Applicants are required to submit a resume, cover letter, online application and 3 professional references. Candidates can submit application materials by clicking on the APPLY NOW button below. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=202087": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Part-Time Shuttle Driver",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Part-Time Shuttle Driver Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Part-Time Shuttle Driver Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Part-Time Shuttle Driver Category: Other Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Miscellaneous &nbsp; Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: Jul 1, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Position ID: 202087 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 11 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2026. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2026 U.S. News rankings: No. 6 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 4 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: GENERAL STATEMENT OF DUTIES: The Part-Time Shuttle Driver is responsible for providing safe, reliable, and professional transportation services for students participating in academic and co-curricular activities. This position operates college-owned vans and transports students to field placements, internships, community engagement sites, and designated shopping destinations in North Adams, Pittsfield, and surrounding areas. The Shuttle Driver serves as an ambassador of the College by fostering a welcoming, student-centered environment while ensuring compliance with all transportation safety regulations and institutional policies. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Safely operate College-owned passenger vans in accordance with all federal, state, and local traffic laws. Transport students to and from field placements, internships, volunteer sites, and other approved educational experiences. Provide scheduled shopping shuttle services to local stores and businesses in North Adams, Pittsfield, and surrounding communities. Conduct pre-trip and post-trip vehicle inspections and report any maintenance concerns or safety issues. Maintain accurate transportation logs, mileage records, and passenger counts. Ensure vehicles remain clean, fueled, and ready for scheduled use. Assist students with boarding and exiting vehicles as needed. Communicate professionally and effectively with students, faculty, staff, and community partners. Respond appropriately to emergencies, incidents, or unexpected travel disruptions. Adhere to all College transportation policies, procedures, and risk management guidelines. Perform other related duties as assigned. Requirements: Required: High school diploma or equivalent. Valid driver's license with a clean driving record. Ability to successfully pass a motor vehicle record review and any required background checks. Demonstrated commitment to safety and customer service. Ability to work independently and exercise sound judgment. Strong interpersonal and communication skills. Preferred: Previous experience driving passenger vans, shuttles, or similar vehicles. Experience working with college students or in an educational setting. Knowledge of Berkshire County roads and surrounding communities. Physical Requirements Ability to sit for extended periods while operating a vehicle. Ability to assist with loading and unloading light items when necessary. Ability to work outdoors in varying weather conditions. Additional Information: This is a part-time, temporary, non-benefitted position starting 9/2/2026 running through 5/14/2027. This position is 15-18 hours per week, requiring evening and weekend availability depending on operational needs. Salary for this position is $18-$21/hour. Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Candidates are required to submit a resume, cover letter, application and 3 professional references. All interested applicants should click the apply now button to submit an application. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
    },
    "http://mcla.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=200067": {
      "title": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Part Time - Assistant Athletic Trainer",
      "description": "Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts\nJob Posting: Part Time - Assistant Athletic Trainer Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts \n\tJob Posting: Part Time - Assistant Athletic Trainer Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Part Time - Assistant Athletic Trainer Category: Support Staff Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Locations: North Adams, MA Posted: May 1, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Part-time Position ID: 200067 Share About Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts: At MCLA, we're here for all, and focused on each of our students. Classes are taught by educators who care deeply about teaching, and about seeing their students thrive on every level of their lives. In every way possible, the experience at MCLA is designed to elevate our students as individuals, leaders, and communicators, fully empowered to make their impressions on the world. In addition to our 130-year commitment to public education, we have fortified our dedication to equitable academic excellence. MCLA has appeared on U.S. News & World Report's list of Top Ten Public Colleges for 11 consecutive years, earning the No. 6 spot on the list of Top Public Liberal Arts Schools in the nation for 2026. The College's focus on affordable education and economic prosperity is reflected in additional 2026 U.S. News rankings: No. 6 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for liberal arts colleges in the state and No. 4 for Top Performer on Social Mobility for public liberal arts colleges in the country. These rankings measure how well schools graduate students who receive Federal Pell Grants. Learn more at www.mcla.edu Job Description: Detailed Statement of Duties: Assistant Athletic Trainer Care for and provide treatment, assessment, rehabilitation, prevention and referral for student-athlete injuries. Maintain and input medical files and notes for student-athletes through SportsWare (EMR). Maintain communication with student-athletes, coaches, and medical staff regarding the care and prevention of athletic injuries Provide athletic training coverage for practices and competitions as appointed by the Director of Athletics and in consultation from the Head Athletic Trainer.  Practice and game coverage will include some nights, weekends, and holidays. Assist with preseason clearances and concussion testing for student-athletes. Administrative Responsibilities Support mission and vision of athletic department Complete all department, College, and conference trainings Conform to the highest standards of professional conduct regarding compliance with department, institutional and conference policies and procedures and adhere to the rules and regulations of athletic governance organizations of which the institution is a member Contribute to the maintenance of good working relationships with all staff, student-athletes, and external constituents through (a) a positive and constructive approach to all tasks, (b) respect for the competencies of others, and (c) appropriate conflict resolution behavior Any staff member who is found in violation of NCAA regulations shall be subject to disciplinary or corrective action set forth in the provisions of the NCAA enforcement procedure and in accordance with the terms of any applicable collective bargaining agreement, and a staff member may be suspended without pay or terminated if the coach is found to be involved in deliberate and serious violations of NCAA regulations. Requirements: Bachelor's degree required, Master's preferred National Athletic Trainers Association (N.A.T.A.) Certifications Massachusetts State License (Board of Registration of Allied Health Professions) or Eligible One to three years of Collegiate Athletic Training or related experience preferred An awareness of higher education principles, practices, and procedures Strong interpersonal skills Ability to express self in written and oral communication Ability to work effectively with professional, non-professional, and student personnel Excellent organizational skills and demonstrated attention to detail Ability to demonstrate appropriate level of initiative Additional Information: MCLA is currently hiring for two Assistant Athletic Trainers These are part-time, temporary, non-benefitted positions starting on 8/10/26 and going through 10/18/26. The Assistant Athletic Trainer will be working up to 18.5 hours per week. Salary for this position is $32.25/hour. Until further notice, in response to the Presidential proclamation, issued on September 19, 2025, imposing a significant fee on the entry of individuals with new H-1B visas, this position is not eligible for visa sponsorship. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) to provide reasonable accommodations to qualified applicants and employees with disabilities. Should you need to request a reasonable accommodation for the application process, please email Brenda Stokes, Executive Director of Human Resources. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts is deeply committed to fostering a community of excellence, equity, diversity, and inclusion. We provide equal access to educational, co-curricular and employment opportunities for all applicants, students, and employees regardless of race, color, religion, national origin, age, disability, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, genetic information, marital or parental status, veteran status, or membership in any other legally protected class. Massachusetts College of Liberal Arts does not discriminate on the basis of sex in any educational program or activity that they operate, which extends to admission and employment. Inquiries about Title IX, Equal Opportunity, or ADA/504 and other non-discrimination policies may be directed to MCLA's Interim Title IX and Equal Opportunity Coordinator, to the Assistant Secretary of the Department of Education Office for Civil Rights, or both. Lucas Morrill Interim Title IX and EO Coordinator 375 Church Street, North Adams, MA 01247 Telephone: (413) 662-5103 Titleix@mcla.edu U.S. Department of Education, Office for Civil Rights 33 Arch Street, 9th Floor Boston, MA 02119-1424 Telephone: (617) 289-0111; FAX: (617) 289-0150; TDD (877) 521-2172 OCR.Boston@ed.gov Application Instructions: Applicants should electronically submit an application by clicking on the APPLY NOW button below. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Massachusetts college of liberal Arts - Main Campus 375 Church Street North Adams, MA 01247"
